Title: The Velveteen Rabbit

Credit: Written by

Author: Margery Williams

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A warm, hand-painted period storybook nursery. THE BOY, six, slight, dark-curled, in a white nightshirt, sits before a stocking. The VELVETEEN RABBIT is wedged at its top: fat and bunchy, brown-and-white spotted velveteen, thread whiskers, pink sateen-lined ears, holly between his paws.

(SOUND: Christmas paper rustles; a toy engine chugs and a clockwork mouse ticks.)

THE BOY
Bunny!

The Boy hugs the VELVETEEN RABBIT close. Nuts, oranges, chocolate almonds, and toys spill from the stocking around them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was quite the best of all. For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles arrive. Tissue paper avalanches across the nursery as new presents are unwrapped. The VELVETEEN RABBIT slips beneath the paper and is forgotten.

(SOUND: tissue paper crackles; adult footsteps and laughter recede down the hall.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then, in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The same nursery. The VELVETEEN RABBIT lies near the fender beside SKIN HORSE, ancient and wise, his brown coat bald in patches, seams showing, tail nearly bare.

(SOUND: a nursery clock ticks; wind-up gears click on a high shelf.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor. The mechanical toys were very superior, and the poor little Rabbit was made to feel insignificant and commonplace.

The mechanical toys turn their painted faces away. SKIN HORSE nudges close to the shy Rabbit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT glances at his smooth fur and polished button eyes.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit leans against the old horse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He wished that he could become it without these uncomfortable things happening to him.

(SOUND: the nursery door clicks shut; the house settles into evening quiet.)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE BOY, six, slight and dark-curled, searches beside a small bed. His china dog is missing. NANA, stout and gray-haired beneath a white apron, hurries through the room. The toy cupboard door stands open, and the VELVETEEN RABBIT is visible inside.

(SOUND: floorboards creak; a night wind rattles the window.)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

NANA drags the VELVETEEN RABBIT out by one ear and places him in the Boy's arms.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy hugs him tight, rolls over on him, and pushes him beneath the pillow. The Rabbit's face is pressed into the darkness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At first he found it rather uncomfortable.

The Boy builds a tunnel beneath the bedclothes. The Rabbit emerges into a warm little burrow beneath the Boy's chin.

(SOUND: bedclothes whisper; muffled giggles and springs creak.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very soon he grew to like it. They had splendid games together, in whispers, when Nana had gone away to her supper.

THE BOY'S hands close around the Rabbit as he sleeps. The Rabbit nestles against his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And when the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit would snuggle down close under his little warm chin and dream, with the Boy's hands clasped close round him all night long.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring sunlight fills the garden. THE BOY, six, slight, dark-curled, now in a red cardigan and short trousers, pushes a wheelbarrow with the VELVETEEN RABBIT aboard. The Rabbit's tail is coming unsewn, and the pink has rubbed from his nose.

(SOUND: the wheelbarrow squeaks; bees hum among the flowers.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

The Rabbit rides through the grass. The Boy spreads a picnic and builds fairy huts beneath the raspberry canes. He tucks the Rabbit into a nest of leaves.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was very happy--so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

Late afternoon. A call from the house draws the Boy away. The VELVETEEN RABBIT remains among the flower beds, surrounded by the little shelters they made together.

(SOUND: distant house bells; the garden insects thicken as the light fades.)

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

The VELVETEEN RABBIT lies on the lawn, wet through with dew and earthy from the flower-bed burrows. NANA, stout, gray-haired, and white-aproned, searches with a candle.

(SOUND: wet grass brushes Nana's shoes; the candle flame trembles in the wind.)

NANA lifts the Rabbit and rubs him with a corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

She carries him toward the house.

(SOUND: the candle glass clinks; a distant clock strikes the hour.)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE BOY, six, slight and dark-curled, sits upright in bed. The damp, earthy VELVETEEN RABBIT rests on the blanket while NANA reaches to take him away.

(SOUND: mattress springs groan; the mantel clock ticks.)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

NANA freezes. The Boy pulls the Rabbit against his chest.

(SOUND: the room falls still except for the clock.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT's once-polished boot-button eyes are dull, but a look of wisdom has come into them.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Morning light pours across the same room. THE BOY, slight and dark-curled, sleeps beside the VELVETEEN RABBIT, whose fur is worn and whose pink-lined ears have begun to turn gray. NANA lifts the old Bunny and studies his face.

(SOUND: birds call beyond the open window; a wash basin rings softly.)

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

The Boy reaches for the Rabbit before Nana can set him down.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was a wonderful Summer.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

A long June evening in the wood. THE BOY, six, slight, dark-curled, in his red cardigan, carries the VELVETEEN RABBIT into the bracken. He makes a little nest and then wanders off among the trees.

(SOUND: cicadas pulse; leaves whisper overhead.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One evening, while the Rabbit was lying there alone, two strange beings crept out of the tall bracken near him.

BRAMBLE, a sleek brown wild rabbit, and FERN, a smaller tawny rabbit, emerge. Their fur is new, their seams invisible. As they move, their bodies stretch long and bunch fat.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT stares, searching for the clockwork that must make them jump.

BRAMBLE
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

BRAMBLE
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

BRAMBLE gives a great sideways hop and stands on his hind legs.

BRAMBLE
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

BRAMBLE
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The VELVETEEN RABBIT sits very still. His back is made all in one piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

BRAMBLE stretches out his neck and looks closely.

BRAMBLE
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

BRAMBLE
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

BRAMBLE whirls and dances. A strange, tickly feeling runs through the VELVETEEN RABBIT.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

BRAMBLE comes close. His whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose and jumps back.

BRAMBLE
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

(SOUND: the Boy's footsteps rush closer; two sets of wild paws drum over the leaves.)

BRAMBLE and FERN vanish into the bracken in a flash of white tails. THE BOY runs past, searching for his toy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The bracken sways empty. Ants travel over the Rabbit's velvet paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why did they run away like that? Why couldn't they stop and talk to me?

The sun sinks lower. White moths flutter out as the Boy returns and carries the Rabbit home.

(SOUND: moth wings flutter; the wood falls quiet around the retreating footsteps.)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks pass. THE BOY, six, slight and dark-curled, lies flushed and restless in bed. The VELVETEEN RABBIT, still brown-and-white but almost shapeless now, lies hidden beneath the bedclothes against him.

(SOUND: feverish breathing; water sloshes in a basin and hurried footsteps cross the hall.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weeks passed, and the little Rabbit grew very old and shabby, but the Boy loved him just as much.

The Boy's hot hand closes around the Rabbit's worn body.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To him he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

NANA and DOCTOR HART, gaunt and gray-whiskered in a dark frock coat, move in and out of the room. A lamp burns through the night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, one day, the Boy was ill.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT stays motionless beneath the blankets as shadows pass over the wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The little Rabbit lay there, hidden from sight, and never stirred, for he knew that the Boy needed him.

(MUSIC: a low, patient lullaby under the ticking clock.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a long weary time.

The fevered Boy turns in sleep. The Rabbit creeps close to the pillow and whispers his garden dreams into the Boy's ear.

(SOUND: the Boy's breathing slowly eases; the clock ticks toward dawn.)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Bright morning fills the same room. THE BOY, still slight and dark-curled but pale from illness, sits wrapped in a shawl on the balcony beyond the open windows. The VELVETEEN RABBIT lies tangled among the bedclothes with only his head peeping out. DOCTOR HART stands beside a packed traveling case.

(SOUND: birds call through the open windows; balcony doors knock softly in the breeze.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently the fever turned, and the Boy got better. He was able to sit up in bed and look at picture-books.

The Boy gazes toward the sunlit garden. A small seaside case waits near the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. Everything was arranged.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah! To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!

NANA catches sight of the Rabbit beneath the bedclothes.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR HART
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

NANA pulls the VELVETEEN RABBIT from the bed and gathers the old picture-books and toys.

(SOUND: the traveling case snaps shut; sackcloth rasps as Nana opens a sack.)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

At the far end of the garden, behind the fowl-house, a rough sack lies beside a pile of old picture-books and rubbish. The VELVETEEN RABBIT is inside, one worn ear visible through the untied mouth.

(SOUND: hens cluck behind the boards; the sack drags over gravel.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so the little Rabbit was put into a sack with the old picture-books and a lot of rubbish, and carried out to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house.

A shovel and a basket of vegetables show that the gardener is too busy to stop. He leaves the sack for the morning.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The gardener was too busy just then to attend to it. Next morning, he promised, he would burn the whole lot.

(SOUND: a shovel scrapes earth; footsteps recede toward the house.)

INT. SECOND BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE BOY, slight and dark-curled, sleeps in a different bedroom, hugging a splendid new bunny of white plush with real glass eyes. A seaside case waits at the foot of the bed.

(SOUND: a small clock ticks; the house settles around the sleeping child.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom, and he had a new bunny to sleep with him. It was a splendid bunny, all white plush with real glass eyes, but the Boy was too excited to care very much about it.

The Boy smiles in his sleep, dreaming of the seaside.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To-morrow he was going to the seaside, and that in itself was such a wonderful thing that he could think of nothing else.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The untied sack shivers in the cold behind the fowl-house. The VELVETEEN RABBIT wriggles his head through the opening. His threadbare coat offers almost no protection.

(SOUND: cold wind moves through the raspberry canes; sackcloth trembles against the earth.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The little Rabbit lay among the old picture-books, and he felt very lonely.

Through the opening, he sees the raspberry thicket where he once played with the Boy.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden--how happy they were.

(MUSIC: a faint, tender memory theme begins.)

In quick storybook glimpses, fairy huts appear beneath the canes, ants cross the Rabbit's paws in the bracken, and the Boy's small hands build a nest around him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of the Skin Horse, so wise and gentle, and all that he had told him. Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

The VELVETEEN RABBIT lowers his head. A real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose.

(SOUND: one tear strikes the dry earth; the night insects hush.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then a strange thing happened.

A slender green stem rises where the tear fell. Emerald leaves unfold around a golden-cup blossom.

(MUSIC: a clear, rising chime as the mysterious flower opens.)

The blossom opens fully. A FAIRY steps from it, lovely, dressed in pearl and dew-drops, with flowers around her neck and in her hair. She gathers the Rabbit into her arms and kisses his damp nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The VELVETEEN RABBIT looks up at her.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It seemed to him that he had seen her face before, but he couldn't think where.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The FAIRY lifts the VELVETEEN RABBIT toward the moonlit wood.

(SOUND: leaves lift in a soft rushing wind as they rise into the night.)

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moonlight silvers the bracken. The FAIRY carries the VELVETEEN RABBIT into an open glade. BRAMBLE, sleek and brown, and FERN, tawny and quick, stop dancing with the other wild rabbits and form a ring around them.

(SOUND: night forest hums; paws stop pattering across the velvet grass.)

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

The FAIRY kisses the VELVETEEN RABBIT and sets him on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The VELVETEEN RABBIT sits quite still, remembering his missing hind legs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He did not know that when the Fairy kissed him that last time she had changed him altogether.

Something tickles the Rabbit's nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: grass brushes his nose; a small hind foot thumps against the ground.)

The Rabbit stares. Brown fur, soft and shining, covers his body. His ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers brush the grass.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instead of dingy velveteen he had brown fur, soft and shiny.

The VELVETEEN RABBIT leaps sideways. He springs, whirls, and dances across the glade with BRAMBLE and FERN.

(SOUND: wild paws drum over the turf; delighted thumps echo between the trees.)

(MUSIC: bright strings burst into a joyous, dancing theme.)

The Rabbit stops and looks for the FAIRY. She has vanished.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring returns. THE BOY, now seven, still slight and dark-curled, plays in the wood behind the house in his red cardigan. Two rabbits creep from the bracken to watch him: one brown all over, the other with strange markings beneath his fur, as though he was once spotted.

(SOUND: spring birds call; bracken rustles in the warm breeze.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Autumn passed and Winter, and in the Spring, when the days grew warm and sunny, the Boy went out to play in the wood behind the house.

The marked rabbit lifts his head. Around his soft nose and round black eyes, something familiar remains.

THE BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Boy turns back to his game, never recognizing him. The Real Rabbit watches the child, then follows BRAMBLE and FERN into the sunlit bracken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

(SOUND: the Boy's shoes scuff the leaves; three rabbits bound softly through the wood.)

FADE OUT.

THE END.
